Subject: wasix: spawn an unchanged WebAssembly file without reading it again

Spawning a plain WebAssembly file read all of it from the filesystem and
hashed it, to find its compiled module in the runtime's cache, however
often the same file had been spawned before. A process that starts the
same binary for each of its children paid that for every child, in
proportion to the size of the binary, however little each child then
did.

The BinFactory keeps the module each path compiled to, with the length
and modification time the file's metadata gave before it was read. A
spawn looks the path's metadata up first, and while both match, starts
the module it has without opening the file. Anything else reads, hashes
and loads the file as before and records what it loaded. The map is
shared by the processes that share the factory, so it lasts as long as
they do, and it holds only modules they spawned. Registering a package
under a name drops any module kept for it, so the package is what that
name then spawns.

The tests check that a module is used while its file is unchanged, that
a rewritten file's is not, and that only an absolute path to a file has
an identity, on a host directory where a relative name finds the same
file.

